Optional<T> is a container object introduced in Java 8 that may or may not contain a non-null value
it helps us handle situations where a value might be missing, reducing the chances of NullPointerException

Important interview points
1. Optional was introduced in Java 8.
2. It belongs to the java.util package.
3. It helps avoid unexpected NullPointerException.
4. It can contain either a non-null value or be empty.
5. It is primarily intended as a return type when a result might be absent.

three main methods to create an Optional:
1. Optional.of()          -  Creates an Optional containing a non-null value
2. Optional.ofNullable()  -  Creates an Optional that may hold a null value
3. Optional.empty()       -  Creates an empty Optional instance

Method	           Input	    Result
of()	           Non-null	    Contains value
of()	           null	        NPE
ofNullable()	   Non-null	    Contains value
ofNullable()	   null	        Empty
empty()            No input 	Empty

1.  isPresent()         	            ifPresent()
    Returns boolean	                    Returns void
    Checks whether value exists	        Executes an action if value exists
    No arguments	                    Accepts Consumer functional interface.
    Used for checking               	Used for performing an action

isPresent()
Checks whether the Optional contains a value.
- Returns true if a value is present.
- Returns false if the Optional is empty.

ifPresent() is useful when you want to perform an action only when a value exists, without explicitly checking for null

2.
orElse()    → evaluates the default argument regardless of whether a value exists.
orElseGet() → When empty calls a Supplier to generate the default value and evaluates the Supplier only when the Optional is empty.
orElseThrow() → returns the value if present. If the Optional is empty, it throws an exception.

Method	        If value is present	    If empty
orElse()	    Returns value	        Returns default
orElseGet() 	Returns value	        Calls Supplier
orElseThrow()	Returns value	        Throws exception

ifPresent & OrElseGet are contradictory to each other. ifPresent is used when you want to perform an action only when a value exists,
while orElseGet is used when you want to provide a default value when the Optional is empty.

3. map() and flatMap() - Both methods are used to transform the value inside an Optional.

    map() transforms a value and wraps the result inside an Optional.
            Optional<Integer> result =  name.map(value -> Optional.of(value.length()));   // This will create an Optional<Optional<Integer>>

    flatMap() transforms a value when the transformation itself returns an Optional, without creating another nested Optional.
            Optional<Integer> result = name.flatMap(value -> Optional.of(value.length()));  // This will create an Optional<Integer>


4. filter() checks whether the value inside an Optional satisfies a given condition.
filter() expects a condition that returns a boolean (true or false).
   - If the condition is true, it keeps the value.
   - If the condition is false, it returns an empty Optional.
   - If the Optional is already empty, the condition is not executed.